---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyAmortization from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyAmortization.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="amortization-schedule-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyAmortization client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What an amortization schedule actually tells you</h2>
    <p>
      A loan quote gives you one number: the monthly payment. An amortization schedule shows each of
      the 360 payments and how much of each one reduces the debt and how much is interest. On a long
      loan, the two parts are very unequal at the start.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a 320,000 loan at 6.25% over thirty years, the payment is 1,970.30. Of the first payment,
      1,666.67 is interest and only 303.63 reduces the balance. Principal is larger than interest only
      from payment 228, late in year nineteen. The balance chart above marks this crossover point for
      your numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula, and why it looks like that</h2>
    <p>
      The level payment on a fixed-rate loan comes from the annuity formula:
    </p>
    <pre><code>M = L × i / (1 − (1 + i)^−n)</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>L</code> is the amount borrowed, <code>i</code> is the monthly interest rate (the annual
      nominal rate divided by twelve) and <code>n</code> is the number of payments. The denominator is
      the present-value factor of an annuity: the single sum today that is worth n payments of one
      unit, discounted at i. The loan divided by this factor is the payment that clears the debt in
      exactly n months.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each month, the schedule does two calculations. Interest is the opening balance times
      <code>i</code>, and the rest of the payment reduces the principal. The shape of the curve, the
      crossover point and the effect of overpayments all come from these two lines.
    </p>

    <h2>Why overpaying works so well</h2>
    <p>
      An extra payment cancels all future interest that the money would have generated. Paying 1,000
      off a 6.25% loan with twenty years left saves the compounding on 1,000 for twenty years, about
      2,450, and not only 62.50.
    </p>
    <p>
      For this reason, the saving is largest early in the loan. The same 1,000 paid in year 28 saves
      almost nothing, because almost no interest is left to cancel. When you overpay, timing matters
      more than size.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Extra per month</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Term becomes</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Interest saved</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>0</td><td style="text-align:right">30 yr 0 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td style="text-align:right">26 yr 3 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">57,725</td></tr>
          <tr><td>250</td><td style="text-align:right">22 yr 4 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">115,679</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500</td><td style="text-align:right">18 yr 1 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">175,303</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td style="text-align:right">13 yr 3 mo</td><td style="text-align:right">238,435</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      For a 320,000 loan at 6.25% over 30 years, with scheduled interest of 389,303. Enter your own
      figures above to get the exact saving and the new payoff date.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you overpay, check two things</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Prepayment penalties: rare on US conventional mortgages, common on some European fixed-rate
        deals, and normal on subprime car finance. Read the agreement.
      </li>
      <li>
        How the payment is applied: many servicers treat unmarked extra money as a prepaid instalment,
        not a principal reduction, and this saves you nothing. You usually must write "apply to
        principal" each time.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Reading the schedule</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Payment: the total that leaves your account, including any extra principal.</li>
      <li>Interest: the opening balance multiplied by the monthly rate.</li>
      <li>Principal: the scheduled part of the payment that reduces the debt.</li>
      <li>Extra: any overpayment, applied to principal in the same month.</li>
      <li>Interest to date: the running total, which is the figure to watch.</li>
      <li>Balance: what you owe after that payment clears.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The yearly tab groups the same data by calendar year, which is the view lenders and tax
      preparers usually want. The Excel export has both views as separate sheets, the CSV export has
      the monthly schedule, and the PDF prints both.
    </p>

    <h2>Precision</h2>
    <p>
      All figures use 40-digit decimal arithmetic, not floating point, and are rounded to the minor
      currency unit once, where a servicer would round. Interest is charged on the opening balance and
      rounded to the cent each month, which is standard for fixed-rate mortgages and instalment loans.
      For a standard fixed-rate loan, the schedule matches a lender statement to the penny.
    </p>
    <p>
      Loans with daily interest, such as some HELOCs and Canadian variable mortgages, differ by a few
      units over a full term. The daily method charges slightly more when payment dates fall late in a
      long month.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing here leaves your device</h2>
    <p>
      The calculation and all three exports run in your browser, so your balance, rate and payoff plan
      are not sent anywhere. To check, open the network tab while you type, or turn off Wi-Fi and
      continue.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
